comments.content:
**The success-path `throw` is immediately swallowed by the `catch (err)` block below it.** Because this line sits inside the same `try { ... }`, throwing `JM_LEDGER_FAILED` here falls straight into `catch (err)`, which:
1. calls `ledgerSafe(...)` again with `status: "error"` — so a run that actually COMPLETED can be recorded as an error row (if the second append happens to succeed, e.g. a transient failure);
2. re-wraps the error as `JM_LEDGER_FAILED: ... (while reporting: JM_LEDGER_FAILED: ...)`, producing a nonsensical message;
3. runs the `if (ledgerFailed)` check in the catch again, re-setting `ledgerFailed` and losing the original reason.

Restructure so the success-path record + failure check happens OUTSIDE the `try/catch` (e.g. assign `result`/`stats`/counters inside a `try` that only wraps `await fn()`, then do the success-path `ledgerSafe(...)` and the `if (ledgerFailed) throw ...` after the `catch` block). Alternatively, tag the error (e.g. a sentinel `name`) and rethrow it unchanged at the top of the `catch`.

